    <h1>Hangfire and background services</h1>
    <p class="docs-lead">What Hangfire is, how it's set up here, how to use its dashboard, what it currently runs (and used to run), and the other work that happens in the background.</p>
    <div class="docs-meta"><span>Dashboard: <code>/hangfire</code></span><span>Code: <code>Program.cs</code>, <code>Components/EventManagement</code>, <code>Components/Logging</code></span></div>
</header>

<h2>What Hangfire is</h2>
<p><a href="https://www.hangfire.io" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Hangfire</a> is a library for running work outside the web request that triggered it: "fire-and-forget" jobs, delayed jobs, and <strong>recurring jobs</strong> on a schedule (cron). Its key idea is that jobs are stored in a database, not in memory, so they survive restarts and can be retried.</p>
<p>It has three parts:</p>
<ul>
    <li><strong>Client</strong>: code that creates a job (for example <code>RecurringJob.AddOrUpdate(...)</code>). The job is saved as the name of a .NET method plus its arguments, serialised as JSON.</li>
    <li><strong>Storage</strong>: database tables that hold jobs, their state, recurring schedules, locks and statistics.</li>
    <li><strong>Server</strong>: a set of worker threads inside the web app that poll storage, pick up due jobs, run the method, and record whether it succeeded. Failed jobs are retried automatically (10 times by default, with increasing delays).</li>
</ul>

